<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nagasaki</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nagasaki is a port city on the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a> in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate</a> it was the only permitted point of contact with European traders, through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> post at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dejima">Dejima</a>, and a centre of controlled Chinese trade. On 9 August 1945 it was struck by an atomic bomb, an event that helped bring about the surrender of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nagasaki.jpg" alt="Nagasaki" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tomio344456 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nagasaki_City_View_from_Glover_Garden,_Nagasaki_2014.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first recorded contact between Portuguese explorers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> came in 1543. In 1569 the daimyō Ōmura Sumitada granted a permit for a port for Portuguese ships at Nagasaki, set up in 1571 under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaspar-vilela">Gaspar Vilela</a>. The harbour village grew into a diverse port city, and Portuguese imports such as tobacco and the sponge-cake castellas entered Japanese culture. In 1587 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> placed it under his direct control and ordered the missionaries expelled, an order largely unenforced.</p><p>On Hideyoshi's order, twenty-six Catholics were crucified at Nagasaki in the following decade. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> was officially banned in 1614, and a brutal persecution followed in which thousands of converts were killed, tortured or forced to renounce their faith. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimabara-rebellion">Shimabara Rebellion</a> of 1637 the Portuguese were expelled, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> were moved from Hirado to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dejima">Dejima</a>, the artificial island in Nagasaki harbour that had been the Portuguese trading post. In 1720 the ban on Dutch books was lifted, and scholars flocked to Nagasaki to study European science and art, a movement called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rangaku">rangaku</a>. Nagasaki became a treaty port in 1859 and later a centre of shipbuilding under Mitsubishi.</p><p>On 9 August 1945 the Boeing B-29 Bockscar, commanded by Major Charles Sweeney, carried a plutonium bomb called Fat Man from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tinian">Tinian</a>. Its primary target was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokura">Kokura</a>, which was hidden by cloud and smoke, so it flew to the secondary target, Nagasaki, where cloud again obscured the city. A gap in the cloud let the crew see a racetrack, and they dropped the bomb on the Urakami Valley, between the Mitsubishi Steel and Arms Works and the Mitsubishi-Urakami Ordnance Works. It exploded at 11:02 a.m. at about 1,800 feet. The dead numbered 35,000, including 150 Japanese soldiers, 6,200 of the 7,500 employees of the Mitsubishi Munitions plant and 24,000 others, among them 2,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koreans">Koreans</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Nagasaki is the capital and largest city of Nagasaki Prefecture, on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a>, and lies at the head of a long bay that forms the best natural harbour on the island. The city was rebuilt after the war, with new churches and temples, and a museum and memorials to the bombing; it remains primarily a port city with a shipbuilding industry. The Hidden Christian Sites in the Nagasaki Region are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nagasaki” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The atomic bombing of Hiroshima on 6 August 1945 and <b>Nagasaki</b> on 9 August, combined with the Soviet declaration of war on 8 August and the rapid destruction of the Kwantung Army in Manchuria, finally gave Emperor Hirohito the external shock he needed to override institutional resistance to…</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After the atomic bombings of Hiroshima and <b>Nagasaki</b>, where many Korean labourers were among the victims, and the Soviet invasion of Manchuria, Japan surrendered on 15 August 1945, ending 35 years of colonial rule; Japanese troops remained in the south until mid-September.</blockquote><a href="/empire/korea-under-japanese-rule">Korea under Japanese rule · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The maritime restrictions later known as sakoku, set out in edicts of the 1630s, banned Japanese from travelling abroad, expelled the Portuguese and confined the Dutch from 1641 to the artificial island of Dejima in <b>Nagasaki</b>, where Chinese merchants also traded.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…Front in 1918, and in the Second World War, which the United States entered after the Japanese attack on Pearl Harbor on 7 December 1941, its industry and forces fought in Europe and the Pacific, and the war ended after atomic bombs were dropped on Hiroshima and <b>Nagasaki</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-states">United States · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Sakamoto Ryōma of Tosa Domain helped Chōshū buy weapons from British merchants through his firm in <b>Nagasaki</b>, brokered the Satsuma–Chōshū Alliance and drafted the Eight Point Plan; he was assassinated in Kyōto after the shōgun’s resignation.</blockquote><a href="/movement/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration · People</a></li><li><blockquote>The plutonium bomb Fat Man exploded over <b>Nagasaki</b>, killing some 40,000 people at once and many more later.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-9">9 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
